WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.660
Let u be any positive integer.

00:00:02.340 --> 00:00:05.840
Work with Gaussian integer coefficients
on the cycle of length u squared.

00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:09.473
Each of the four polynomials
H zero through H three

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:12.980
has a positive or negative one
at every coefficient position.

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:16.320
Keep those same signs in all
three normalized evaluations:

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:23.367
c is half the sum of all four polynomials;
d reverses the signs on H one and H three;

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:26.867
and g uses the real difference
of H zero and H two,

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:31.280
plus i times the difference of H
one and H three, all divided by two.

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:36.660
Subtract c from d and from g. The H
zero terms cancel in both differences.

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:40.460
Our divisions will be exact,
before any coefficient reduction.

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:44.320
First, subtract the two real formulas.

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:49.453
The difference d minus c is
negative H one minus H three.

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:54.720
Dividing by two gives negative one
half of the sum of H one and H three.

00:00:55.060 --> 00:00:58.840
For the second quotient, the
difference g minus c has numerator

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:02.427
negative H one minus
twice H two minus H three,

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:07.060
plus i times the difference of
H one and H three, over two.

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:11.800
To divide by one plus i,
multiply by one minus i over two.

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:17.010
Expanding the numerator gives real part
negative twice H two minus twice H three,

00:01:17.195 --> 00:01:21.140
and imaginary part twice H one
plus twice H two, all over four.

00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:25.513
Simplify to negative one half
of the sum of H two and H three,

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:30.380
plus i times one half of
the sum of H one and H two.

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:32.900
Each half-sum has integer coefficients:

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:38.840
two signs add to negative two, zero, or
two, and their halves are negative one,

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:43.340
zero, or one. Therefore the first
quotient is an integer polynomial,

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:46.340
and the second is a
Gaussian integer polynomial.

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:49.540
Both divisions are
justified before reduction.

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:54.200
Now define E as the sum of
the two integral quotients.

00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:56.060
Add their exact formulas.

00:01:56.700 --> 00:02:01.360
The two terms negative H three over
two combine to negative H three.

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:07.047
The remaining real terms are negative
one half of the sum of H one and H two;

00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:11.220
the imaginary terms are i
times that same half-sum.

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:16.653
Collect the common factor i minus one.
We obtain E equal to negative H three,

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:22.440
plus the factor i minus one times
the half-sum of H one and H two.

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:27.800
This isolates the correction term.
Since i minus one equals i times the

00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:32.060
quantity one plus i, that correction
will vanish in the later reduction.

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:35.060
We have not performed that reduction here.

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:40.000
Retain the two exact divisibilities
as well: two divides d minus c,

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:45.320
and one plus i divides g minus c. These
calculations use the common signs alone.

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:49.380
They require neither norm
identities nor oddness of u.
